<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#west-indies-associated-states">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Indies Associated States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1967–1983</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> Associated States was the collective name for British colonies in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> that became states in free association with the United Kingdom under the West Indies Act 1967. Each had full internal self-government, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept responsibility for defence and foreign affairs. Antigua, Saint Christopher-Nevis-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a>, Dominica, Grenada and Saint Lucia took the status in 1967 and Saint Vincent in 1969; each left it on independence, the last in 1983.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-indies-associated-states.jpg" alt="West Indies Associated States" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_coat_of_arms_of_the_United_Kingdom_(1952%E2%80%932022,_variant_1).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
